From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zaid <283011337+nottzaid@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Alpha-masked foliage

PBRAppearance.alphaCutoff (glTF MASK): texels below it are cut, not
blended; alpha is scaled by mip level so distant foliage keeps its
coverage. Blending was slower and could mis-sort foliage (the source's
53 materials are all alpha-hashed). Alpha-masked materials draw both
faces, back faces shaded towards the viewer, as Blender does:
single-faced needle cards lost half their cards.

Minified normal maps no longer take a frame from screen derivatives,
which breaks once a pixel spans several needles: the mipmapped normal
is used as is, and its length (how much its texels disagree) darkens
the cosine. A first-order model (1/4 + n.l/2) lit needles facing away
from the sun by a quarter of it: backlit crowns glowed. A degenerate
normal-map frame (tiny triangles in a pixel quad) falls back to the
surface normal instead of NaN.

Replaces these patches of the numbered series, with what each measured
as it landed (tools/profile_forest.py, GTX 1060 3 GB, i5-4670):
- 0004-pbr-alpha-cutoff: frame 31 -> 22 ms (shadows off)
- 0016-foliage-shading: distant trees' direct light within 4% of Cycles
  (was 27% dark)
- 0032-backlit-foliage (its foliage part)
- 0037-normal-map-degenerate-frame
